nippet\\\": \\\"The meaning of OPEN is having no enclosing or confining barrier : accessible on all or nearly all sides. How to use open in a sentence. Synonym Discussion of Open.\\\"}, {\\\"title\\\": \\\"The home of free learning from The Open University\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/\\\", \\\"snippet\\\": \\\"Everything on the multi-award winning OpenLearn is free to everyone! With hundreds of courses, articles, videos and interactives, OpenLearn has options for all. Earn a free digital badge or statement of participation on most courses. Refresh your study skills and prepare for student life.\\\"}, {\\\"title\\\": \\\"Restaurants and Restaurant Bookings | OpenTable\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://secure.opentable.com/\\\", \\\"snippet\\\": \\\"OpenTable is a real-time online booking network for fine dining restaurants.\\\"}, {\\\"title\\\": \\\"Opendoor Technologies Inc. (OPEN) Stock Price, News, Quote & History ...\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/OPEN/\\\", \\\"snippet\\\": \\\"Find the latest Opendoor Technologies Inc. (OPEN) stock quote, history, news and other vital information to help you with your stock trading and investing.\\\"}, {\\\"title\\\": \\\"open (2) - Linux manual page - man7.org\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/open.2.html\\\", \\\"snippet\\\": \\\"The dirfd argument for these APIs can be obtained by using open () or openat () to open a directory (with either the O_RDONLY or the O_PATH flag). Alternatively, such a file descriptor can be obtained by applying dirfd (3) to a directory stream created using opendir (3).\\\"}, {\\\"title\\\": \\\"Open - Definition, Meaning & Synonyms | Vocabulary.com\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://www.vocabulary.com/dictionary/open\\\", \\\"snippet\\\": \\\"The adjective open describes something that's not closed or blocked up, like open curtains that provide a view of the garden outside. Open can also refer to something that's unfastened or parted — an open jar of peanut butter, an open eye, or an open book.\\\"}]}\", \"excerpt_truncated\": false, \"source_sha256\": \"a6716bd1b99c58a68ecf4eb50579374d8a87a46419e3ce1e01e180dd0392d0fe\", \"verification_required\": true, \"topic_domain\": \"unprompted_complexity\", \"evidence_role\": \"discovery\", \"host_tier\": \"discovery\", \"source_identity\": null, \"persistent_identifiers\": [], \"source_candidates\": [\"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/\"], \"source_candidate_identities\": {}}", "id": "source-e458d792406a4d40", "scope": "collected", "source": "https://www.bing.com/search?q=open+ended+learning+emergent+complexity+adaptive+systems&format=rss"}, "prev_hash": "a8da935579b411f143dc1ac6d096f59064049a14a6695073aa2ae9858ac7e8d4", "hash": "a83884665c0cfa968fcebf7c3978da7eae414a20eef4789d24d8edbb3dad1daa"}, {"seq": 58, "time": "2026-10-27T12:20:25.812198+00:00", "kind": "research_planned", "payload": {"id": "w-af480920b16b452f", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 13212, "provider_input_hash": "28fd25d1fe6b09d50094c94f2d8adb623c5875e3e4417ffbc66c4e24a9042e23", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 112.50928282737732}, {"elapsed_ms": 937, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 13212, "provider_input_hash": "28fd25d1fe6b09d50094c94f2d8adb623c5875e3e4417ffbc66c4e24a9042e23", "request_payload_bytes": 14352, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 66, "promptTokenCount": 3086, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 3086}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 3152}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}], "provider_requests_sent": 1, "successful_model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "usage": {"candidat